    "category": "Crop Health, Diseases & Pesticide Guidance",
    "question": "My crop leaves are turning yellow. What should I do?",
    "answer": "Work through it in this order. (1) Uniform yellowing starting on the older, lower leaves, moving upward = nitrogen deficiency -- correct with a urea top-dress. (2) Yellowing/browning starting at leaf tips and margins = potassium deficiency or plain moisture stress. (3) Yellowing between the veins on new leaves while veins stay green = iron/ manganese deficiency, common in high-pH or waterlogged soil. (4) Yellowing in patches with stunted growth, especially with a whitefly/aphid population nearby = a possible viral infection -- remove and destroy affected plants, don't wait. (5) Yellowing right after heavy rain/irrigation, with wilting despite wet soil = root suffocation from waterlogging, not a nutrient issue at all -- improve drainage first.",
