    "id": 18,
    "category": "Crop Health, Diseases & Pesticide Guidance",
    "question": "My tomato leaves have spots. What is the problem?",
    "answer": "Three common culprits: early blight (Alternaria solani) -- brown spots with concentric \"target-ring\" patterns, starting on older leaves; Septoria leaf spot -- many small circular spots with dark margins and grey centers, also lower leaves first; bacterial spot -- small water- soaked spots that turn brown and scabby, often with a yellow halo. For the fungal two, remove and destroy infected leaves, avoid wetting foliage when watering, and apply mancozeb or chlorothalonil at first sign. For bacterial spot, use a copper-based bactericide instead -- fungicides won't touch it. Rotate out of tomato/other solanaceous crops on that plot next season either way.",
