    "answer": "Sort them first, because the fix is different for each: (1) Sucking pests -- aphids, whiteflies, jassids, thrips -- cause curling, yellowing, sticky honeydew and black sooty mold; yellow sticky traps help monitor them, and a systemic insecticide like imidacloprid or thiamethoxam (or neem oil for light infestations) controls them. (2) Chewing/defoliating pests -- caterpillars, beetles -- leave visible holes or skeletonized leaves; a targeted insecticide like chlorantraniliprole, or a Bt-based biopesticide, works well and is gentler on beneficial insects. (3) Borers -- stem or fruit borers -- show as entry holes with visible frass; pheromone traps plus a recommended insecticide timed to early larval stage work best. Scout twice a week and only spray once the pest count crosses the economic threshold for that crop -- not automatically at first sighting.",
